==Russian==

===Etymology 1===
OCS [[градъ]] from PIE *grōd-/*grǝd- or *ghrōdh-/*ghrǝdh-. Cognate with Lithuanian [[grúodas]] (Balto-Slavic *grōda-), Latin [[grando|grandō]], Armenian [[կարկուտ]] (karkut, from Old Armenian *gagrōdo-), Old Indic *hrādúniṣ, and possibly with English [[grind]].

====Noun====
'''град''' (grad) {{m}}

# [[hail]]
#:*Град [[идти|идёт]] — ''It’s hailing.''
# [[shower]], [[rain]]
# [[volley]], [[shower]]
#:*град [[пуля|пуль]] — ''hail of bullets''

=====Declension=====
{{ru-noun1|град||гра́да||гра́ду||град||гра́дом||о гра́де|}}

===Etymology 2===
OCS [[градъ]] from Proto-Indo-European '''*gharto-''', from PIE base '''*gher-''' (to enclose, to grasp), either directly or possibly via Proto-Germanic '''*garda'''. Cognate with English [[yard]], [[garden]], [[gird]], German [[Garten]], Danish [[gård]], Norwegian [[gard]].

